WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.334
GRADY POWELL: Brian,

00:00:02.401 --> 00:00:04.404
Both of you guys are moving
forward into the next round.

00:00:04.471 --> 00:00:05.905
In round three of
the competition,

00:00:05.972 --> 00:00:07.840
we're going to be sending you
guys back to your home forges

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:10.076
to build an iconic
weapon from history.

00:00:10.142 --> 00:00:17.083
That weapon is the
sword of Saladin.

00:00:17.116 --> 00:00:19.853
This 12th century sword
belonged to Saladin,

00:00:19.919 --> 00:00:22.688
a powerful Muslim leader
and the first sultan

00:00:22.755 --> 00:00:23.990
of Egypt and Syria.

00:00:24.057 --> 00:00:26.492
Believed to have been forged
from steel far superior

00:00:26.559 --> 00:00:28.428
than any other
weapon of the time,

00:00:28.495 --> 00:00:30.763
the sword is rumored to be
the sharpest in history.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:33.999
Featuring a thin edged geometry,
the lightweight, curved blade

00:00:34.066 --> 00:00:35.935
is designed to
effortlessly deliver

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:37.970
deep cuts and lethal chops.

00:00:38.037 --> 00:00:39.505
With the help of
this deadly weapon,

00:00:39.572 --> 00:00:42.675
Saladin founded a dynasty that
ruled much of the Middle East

00:00:42.741 --> 00:00:44.643
for the 12th and 13th centuries.

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:47.380
The sword is a prominent feature
of a statue memorializing

00:00:47.446 --> 00:00:51.017
Saladin that stands in front
of the ancient city of Damascus

00:00:51.084 --> 00:00:52.952
in Syria.

00:00:53.018 --> 00:00:54.653
WALTER: I'm absolutely excited.

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:56.789
And it's a lot longer than
what I normally build,

00:00:56.856 --> 00:00:58.357
but yeah, we can do this.

00:00:58.424 --> 00:00:59.825
GRADY POWELL: You
guys will only have

00:00:59.892 --> 00:01:01.294
four days to
complete this weapon,

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:02.628
so we'll see when you get back.

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:03.396
Good luck.

00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:10.102
We're back here,
[inaudible] liked the tool.

00:01:10.169 --> 00:01:11.837
In good old
Springfield, New York,

00:01:11.904 --> 00:01:13.039
I'm ready to get after it.

00:01:13.106 --> 00:01:15.575
My biggest concern
is time management.

00:01:15.641 --> 00:01:18.911
When you're on a timed
challenge like this,

00:01:18.978 --> 00:01:20.813
every minute is precious.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.014
Curve's going pretty well.

00:01:22.048 --> 00:01:23.482
I'm done with my forging.

00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:26.285
Head straight to the grinder and
start cleaning this thing up,

00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:28.621
make it look right.

00:01:28.688 --> 00:01:29.489
Time to quench.

00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:39.099
That's the sound of
success right there.

00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:42.868
BRIAN: Back at my home forge.

00:01:42.935 --> 00:01:46.172
Making a sword with a bend in it
and such a steep arc like this

00:01:46.205 --> 00:01:48.841
is worrying me a little bit.

00:01:48.908 --> 00:01:51.977
I've not done a heat treat on
something of this shape before.

00:01:52.044 --> 00:01:54.046
We're close.

00:01:54.079 --> 00:01:55.948
I'm a little worried
about the heat treat.

00:01:56.015 --> 00:01:56.783
And away we go.

00:02:03.323 --> 00:02:05.491
Oh yeah.

00:02:05.558 --> 00:02:07.059
That's it.

00:02:07.126 --> 00:02:08.027
I got the curling on it.

00:02:08.094 --> 00:02:09.462
I like it.

00:02:09.528 --> 00:02:12.098
And I got the pommel built,
really much the shape I want.

00:02:12.164 --> 00:02:14.433
This is a emerald-dyed
box elder burl.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:18.204
I want it to look jeweled
without actually being jeweled.

00:02:18.271 --> 00:02:20.739
We'll get everything
pretty well rough fit here.

00:02:20.773 --> 00:02:23.943
I'm just kind of pecking
away at it, going slowly.

00:02:23.977 --> 00:02:25.878
Nobody is going to
be able to complain

00:02:25.945 --> 00:02:28.381
about that handle at all.

00:02:28.447 --> 00:02:31.116
This has been a hell of a
ride, but I hope that can

00:02:31.183 --> 00:02:32.585
bring me home the bacon.

00:02:32.652 --> 00:02:35.754
DOUG MARCAIDA: Bladesmiths,
welcome to the kill test.

00:02:35.821 --> 00:02:38.557
To find what kind of lethal
damage your weapons will do,

00:02:38.624 --> 00:02:41.227
I will take your version
of Saladin's sword

00:02:41.261 --> 00:02:44.730
and deliver some lethal
blows on this ram carcass.

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:45.565
Brian, you're up first.

00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:46.699
You ready for this?

00:02:46.766 --> 00:02:48.634
 No, but I guess you
gotta do what you gotta do.

00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:49.569
 Absolutely.

00:02:49.602 --> 00:02:50.369
Let's do it.

00:02:50.436 --> 00:02:51.837
BRIAN: To see that
animal sitting

00:02:51.904 --> 00:02:56.241
there with the horns hanging
out, it's nerve-wracking.

00:02:56.308 --> 00:02:58.244
It's pretty hard on
a blade, so I'm not

00:02:58.277 --> 00:02:59.445
sure what's going to happen.

00:03:17.363 --> 00:03:19.999
DOUG MARCAIDA: All right, Brian,
let's talk about your version

00:03:20.032 --> 00:03:21.400
of Saladin's sword here.

00:03:21.434 --> 00:03:23.603
I notice you have something
inscribed in there.

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:24.503
What is that?

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:26.238
BRIAN: That is
"Saladin" in Arabic.

00:03:26.305 --> 00:03:27.707
DOUG MARCAIDA: Very
cool, very cool.

00:03:27.774 --> 00:03:31.644
Now, your edge-- you
have a sharp edge.

00:03:31.711 --> 00:03:34.347
This is a very
tough ram carcass.

00:03:34.414 --> 00:03:36.549
On the neck chops,
it cut deeply.

00:03:36.582 --> 00:03:40.353
And of course, on the body, it
cut through in three slices.

00:03:40.419 --> 00:03:44.991
Overall, sir, your
weapon will kill.

00:03:45.058 --> 00:03:46.058
 Thank you, sir.

00:03:46.125 --> 00:03:47.193
 All right, Walter,
your turn, sir.

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:48.161
Are you ready?

00:03:48.194 --> 00:03:49.361
 Let's make some ram chops.

00:03:49.428 --> 00:03:50.629
DOUG MARCAIDA: All right.

00:03:50.696 --> 00:03:53.265
WALTER: Them bones in a
ram, those are no joke.

00:03:53.298 --> 00:03:55.534
Their hides are as
tough as leather.

00:03:55.567 --> 00:03:58.037
Their fur is abrasive
like sandpaper.

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:00.506
This is a legit test.

00:04:00.573 --> 00:04:03.309
I'm slightly freaking out
on the inside right now.

00:04:14.253 --> 00:04:15.388
 [inaudible].

00:04:18.091 --> 00:04:21.060
 All right, Walter,
first up, your edge.

00:04:21.126 --> 00:04:24.130
This is a wicked sharp grind.

00:04:24.197 --> 00:04:25.965
[inaudible] sings
to me when they

00:04:26.031 --> 00:04:28.000
just touch it and pull it out.

00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:29.335
It is sharp.

00:04:29.402 --> 00:04:33.673
Cutting through hair, a thick
spine, and into the meat.

00:04:33.739 --> 00:04:37.310
Overall, sir, your
weapon will kill.

00:04:37.377 --> 00:04:38.278
 Thank you, sir.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:42.047
DAVID BAKER: All
right, gentlemen,

00:04:42.114 --> 00:04:43.082
you know what time it is.

00:04:43.115 --> 00:04:44.817
It's time for the strength test.

00:04:44.884 --> 00:04:46.886
Now, Saladin's army
in the 12th century

00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:50.189
would have faced off against
men wearing chain mail, carrying

00:04:50.255 --> 00:04:51.356
shields, and probably a helmet.

00:04:51.423 --> 00:04:53.760
Now, to test your
blade's construction,

00:04:53.793 --> 00:04:55.928
overall durability,
I'll be hacking

00:04:55.994 --> 00:04:58.431
into our armored warriors here.

00:04:58.497 --> 00:04:59.665
Brian, you're up.

00:04:59.732 --> 00:05:00.500
 Go ahead.

00:05:08.641 --> 00:05:09.575
 Wow.

00:05:09.608 --> 00:05:10.876
 Foof.

00:05:10.943 --> 00:05:13.612
 All right, Brian, blades
this sharp and with this kind

00:05:13.679 --> 00:05:17.550
of edge geometry often take
a fairly substantial rolling

00:05:17.616 --> 00:05:20.119
when they go up against armor--

00:05:20.186 --> 00:05:21.354
yours really didn't.

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:24.389
I mean there's three
spots I can feel if I

00:05:24.456 --> 00:05:26.024
run my fingernail along it.

00:05:26.091 --> 00:05:26.959
Just a beautiful job.

00:05:27.026 --> 00:05:27.893
Well done.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:28.661
 Thank you, sir.

00:05:28.727 --> 00:05:29.996
 All right, Walter, you're up.

00:05:30.063 --> 00:05:30.763
 Let's do it.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:31.531
DAVID BAKER: OK.

00:05:38.771 --> 00:05:40.139
 Wow.

00:05:40.172 --> 00:05:42.808
 Nice.

00:05:42.875 --> 00:05:45.544
 All right, Walter,
there's a little bit

00:05:45.577 --> 00:05:47.680
of a glinting and a
little bit of a rough spot

00:05:47.714 --> 00:05:50.549
here, here, and maybe
here, but other than that,

00:05:50.616 --> 00:05:53.552
this blade still has
a wicked edge on it.

00:05:53.619 --> 00:05:55.420
Curvature is
really, really nice.

00:05:55.487 --> 00:05:58.190
You nailed it, scale
right on, well done.

00:05:58.257 --> 00:05:59.057
 Thank you, sir.

00:05:59.124 --> 00:05:59.759
DAVID BAKER: You bet.

00:06:03.161 --> 00:06:05.798
 (WHISPERING) Close race.

00:06:05.831 --> 00:06:09.301
All right, bladesmiths,
welcome to the sharpness test.

00:06:09.368 --> 00:06:11.804
To find out how sharp
your weapons are,

00:06:11.837 --> 00:06:13.706
I will take your
weapon and I will cut

00:06:13.739 --> 00:06:16.208
through these Egyptian rugs.

00:06:16.274 --> 00:06:18.310
Now, I'm looking for clean cuts.

00:06:18.344 --> 00:06:19.745
Brian, you're up first.
You ready?

00:06:19.812 --> 00:06:20.479
 Yes, sir.

00:06:20.545 --> 00:06:21.514
DOUG MARCAIDA: Let's do this.

00:06:35.061 --> 00:06:38.164
All right, Brian, let's
talk about your blade here.

00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:40.332
It is razor sharp.

00:06:40.365 --> 00:06:42.668
As I punctured and cut
through the carpet,

00:06:42.735 --> 00:06:44.504
there were very clean cuts.

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:47.873
Overall, sir, your
edge, it will cut.

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:49.041
 Thank you.

00:06:49.107 --> 00:06:50.208
 All right, Walter, you ready?

00:06:50.275 --> 00:06:50.976
 Absolutely.

00:06:51.043 --> 00:06:51.811
Let's do this.

00:07:00.753 --> 00:07:02.488
[ringing sound]

00:07:02.555 --> 00:07:03.556
Listen to that ring.

00:07:08.794 --> 00:07:12.764
 All right, Walter, let's
talk about your weapon here--

00:07:12.831 --> 00:07:13.899
razor sharp.

00:07:13.932 --> 00:07:18.337
It was easy to penetrate,
very easy to drag across.

00:07:18.404 --> 00:07:20.739
These are very clean cuts.

00:07:20.806 --> 00:07:23.876
Overall, sir, your
weapon, it will cut.

00:07:23.943 --> 00:07:25.711
 Thank you, sir.

00:07:25.778 --> 00:07:27.647
GRADY POWELL: All right,
gentlemen, the judges

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:29.114
have made the decision.

00:07:29.181 --> 00:07:35.120
Today's champion is Walter.

00:07:35.187 --> 00:07:36.489
 What, what?

00:07:36.555 --> 00:07:37.757
I'm just in shock.

00:07:37.823 --> 00:07:39.891
[laughs] I can't believe this.

00:07:39.925 --> 00:07:41.660
GRADY POWELL: Now,
unfortunately, Brian, that

00:07:41.693 --> 00:07:42.928
means you did not win today.

00:07:42.995 --> 00:07:44.497
DAVID BAKER: This is
the closest decision

00:07:44.563 --> 00:07:46.665
I have ever had to make, ever.

00:07:46.732 --> 00:07:51.036
Brian, you brought us an
absolutely beautiful weapon.

00:07:51.103 --> 00:07:52.671
It tested fantastically.

00:07:52.704 --> 00:07:54.072
It felt fantastic in my hand.

00:07:54.139 --> 00:07:58.010
We made this decision based
on the smallest of details,

00:07:58.077 --> 00:08:00.947
and it came down to the
curvature of the blade.

00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:04.884
Yours just has a little wobble
in it, the other doesn't--

00:08:04.951 --> 00:08:05.851
it's that close.

00:08:05.917 --> 00:08:08.020
You should be incredibly
proud of that blade,

00:08:08.086 --> 00:08:11.423
and it was an absolute pleasure
to be able to wield it.

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:13.259
 Thank you very much.

00:08:13.326 --> 00:08:14.326
 Brian, unfortunately
that means

00:08:14.392 --> 00:08:15.828
you didn't make the cut today.

00:08:15.861 --> 00:08:18.130
And I'm going to have to ask
you to head off the forge floor.

00:08:18.197 --> 00:08:19.865
 Bro, it's been an honor.

00:08:19.931 --> 00:08:22.468
I'm a little disappointed to
not be in the winner's circle,

00:08:22.534 --> 00:08:23.935
but very proud of
the fact that Dave

00:08:24.002 --> 00:08:25.570
said it was one of
the hardest decisions

00:08:25.604 --> 00:08:26.571
they've ever had to make.

00:08:26.605 --> 00:08:29.675
I feel I did pretty good.

00:08:29.742 --> 00:08:32.311
GRADY POWELL: Well, Walter,
congratulations, buddy.

00:08:32.378 --> 00:08:34.747
You are the Forge of Fire champ,
and you'll be going out of here

00:08:34.814 --> 00:08:36.482
with a check for $10,000.

00:08:36.549 --> 00:08:37.483
How do you feel?

00:08:37.549 --> 00:08:39.818
 I don't even know right now.

00:08:39.885 --> 00:08:41.020
I won.

00:08:41.087 --> 00:08:42.354
I can't believe it.

00:08:42.421 --> 00:08:44.590
$10,000 is nothing
to sneeze at, but I

00:08:44.624 --> 00:08:47.225
want to take a portion of it
and donate it to some kind

00:08:47.292 --> 00:08:48.661
of cancer organization.

00:08:48.728 --> 00:08:51.163
Cancer has taken a lot of
people away from me that

00:08:51.229 --> 00:08:53.465
I care about-- it took my dad--

00:08:53.532 --> 00:08:56.903
and I want to be able to
give back a little bit.

00:08:56.969 --> 00:08:57.970
What a wild ride.

00:08:58.037 --> 00:09:00.806
[music playing]

